# Session continuity checkpoints keep the branch's own version on merge.
#
# .claude/STATE.md and .claude/HANDOFF.md describe what one session did. Several
# agents write them concurrently, so every merge presented two full rewrites of
# the same lines and conflicted — twice in one evening on the same two files,
# each time with nothing real to reconcile. A checkpoint from another branch was
# never the right answer for this branch anyway.
#
# `merge=ours` needs a driver registered per clone; git does not ship one under
# that name (`-s ours` is a whole-merge strategy, not a per-file driver). The
# driver is the shell builtin `true`, which succeeds without touching %A, so the
# current branch's version survives:
#
#   git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# scripts/preflight.sh reports when that is missing. Without it git falls back to
# a normal three-way merge and these files conflict again — noisily, not silently.
#
# Trade-off: after a merge the checkpoint describes the branch you were on, not
# the work you merged in, so it can be stale. scripts/check_continuity_state.py
# compares head_sha against HEAD and fails loudly when that happens, which is the
# failure mode to prefer.
.claude/STATE.md    merge=ours
.claude/HANDOFF.md  merge=ours
